Relative to homography, dense geometry gained nine correct moved-camera removal decisions in Isaac Sim and lost none. It gained sixteen and lost one in MuJoCo (@primary-table). On the tabletop, the scores rose from 30/40 to 39/40 in Isaac and from 30/40 to 40/40 in MuJoCo. Six of eight Isaac tabletop layouts and seven of eight MuJoCo tabletop layouts had positive net gains. On the shelf, the scores were 37/40 for both methods in Isaac and rose from 33/40 to 38/40 in MuJoCo.

The wider set of interventions exposed a cost that the primary endpoint cannot measure. Across all states, dense geometry gained 26 correct decisions and lost 16 in Isaac. It gained 38 and lost 27 in MuJoCo. On the MuJoCo shelf it introduced two false removals of a retained marker in different layouts. One occurred with the wide panel in place. The other occurred with the blocker parked, when 1,518 native target pixels remained visible. In a third layout, its abstention avoided the homography's false removal of a retained marker. The fresh comparison therefore met the primary-gain and tabletop-layout criteria in both engines and failed the zero-new-error criterion in MuJoCo.
